    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d208">

                <pb facs="0207" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/17229: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-09T15:00:00+03:00">December 9, 1941—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:57 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2031. Embassy’s 2022, December 8, noon.<anchor n="78" xml:id="d208-f1"/> The
                    British Ambassador has informed me that Eden, accompanied by a staff of military
                    and other advisers, is expected to arrive in the Soviet Union within the next
                    few days and to proceed directly to Moscow. Cripps, General Mason McFarlane,
                    Admiral Miles<anchor n="79" xml:id="d208-f2"/> and the Counselor and several
                    Secretaries of the Embassy will leave Kuibyshev shortly to meet Eden in Moscow
                    and to participate in his conference with Stalin and other Soviet officials.</p>
                <p>Cripps stated that the impending Anglo-Soviet discussions will, of course, deal
                    with problems related to the general strategy of the present war but that they
                    are designed primarily to work out an understanding with the Soviet Government
                    respecting postwar aims.</p>
                <pb facs="0208" n="196" xml:id="pg196"/>
                <p>In this connection the Ambassador said that he does not feel that any attempt
                    should be made to modify the agreement recently reached<anchor n="80"
                        xml:id="d208-f3"/> between Sikorski<anchor n="81" xml:id="d208-f4"/> and
                    Stalin (Embassy’s 2024, December 8, 2 p.m.<anchor n="82" xml:id="d208-f5"/>)
                    inasmuch as in Sikorski’s opinion by yielding with regard to the retention of
                    the Polish forces in the Soviet Union he had assured Stalin’s full acceptance of
                    the principle that a Polish State should be reconstituted. Cripps added that
                    Stalin had not only indicated his acceptance of this principle but had expressed
                    his desire to see “a great and powerful Polish State” established.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d208-f1" n="78" xml:id="d208-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d208-f2" n="79" xml:id="d208-f2-content">
                <p>Rear Adm. G. J. A. Miles, member of the British Military Mission to the Soviet
                    Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d208-f3" n="80" xml:id="d208-f3-content">
                <p>A Declaration of Friendship and Mutual Assistance was signed between Poland and
                    the Soviet Union in Moscow on December 4, 1941. A text was sent in telegram No.
                    2018, December 6, from The Chargé in the Soviet Union, <ref target="#pg266">p.
                        266</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d208-f4" n="81" xml:id="d208-f4-content">
                <p>Gen. Wladyslaw Sikorski, Prime Minister of the Polish Government in Exile at
                    London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d208-f5" n="82" xml:id="d208-f5-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi>, <ref target="#pg267">p. 267</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
